                <pb facs="0543" n="445" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg445"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3394: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Fuller</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-17T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 17, 1920—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 17—9:56
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of July 16, 7 p.m. Both of the opposing parties express
                    willingness to permit passenger trains. Train leaves here for Peking this
                    evening. Telegraphic communication is reopened via Mukden. … Chihli troops
                    repulsed yesterday at Yangtsun have been heavily reenforced today. Order is
                    being maintained in Tientsin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fuller</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
